<p class='text'>I can remember when I was younger thinking I was quite smart.  I have a sense other people did too.</p>
<p class='text'>But one of the things that I felt I understood that nobody else around me did was the (seemingly very simple?) idea that things that are going to happen sooner are easier to predict than things that are not going to happen for a while.  And, concomitant with that, things that are under your control are a lot easier to predict than things that are dependent on other people or events.</p>
<p class='text'>So (in some order): what I am going to have for lunch today is an easy decision; where "we" as a group are going to go out to on Friday evening is harder; making family holiday plans for next summer is harder still; figuring out which job to take and which house to buy are starting to get really complicated; and then there are the big ones.</p>
<p class='text'>I remember in my 20s frequently telling people that the two hardest decisions I thought I would ever make in my life are who to marry and which pension to invest in.  People would look at me as if I were mental: you marry the most beautiful woman who will talk to you; and you invest in any pension because they're all the same.</p>
<p class='text'>It's a different way of looking at the world, and to me smacks of  <a href='https://www.techtarget.com/whatis/definition/Parkinsons-law-of-triviality-bikeshedding'>bikeshedding</a>, although I don't think we used that word at the time (I had read my Dad's copy of  <a href='https://www.amazon.co.uk/Parkinsons-Law-Pursuit-Progress-Classics/dp/0141186852'>Parkinson</a>).  The complexity involved in these decisions exceeds what any human can possibly cope with, so just do <i>something</i>, otherwise you'll end up in  <a href='https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Analysis_paralysis'>analysis paralysis</a>.</p>
<p class='text'>But I do remember trying to think my way through these problems, both of which I described as "forty year" problems: that is, at age 25, you are expected to make a decision whose consequences will be mainly, significantly or entirely felt only 40 years later, when you are 65.  Ironically, even <i>that</i> has changed with regard to the pension: the retirement age in the UK has changed from 65 to 68 since I bought my pension.</p>
<p class='text'>Let's take the pension first, because it is, incredibly, the easier decision.  Why?  Because it is at least <b>your</b> decision, in a way that a marriage must ever and always be a joint dance.</p>
<p class='text'>In order to buy any investment product you need to consider two things: what do you think the world (and your needs) are going to be when it matures, and how will this product compare to others you could buy?  Just about the only thing I knew was my parents' advice (and what I think was received wisdom) that the more money you put into your pension sooner, the more it would grow, along with some statistic like "the first five years are worth as much as the rest put together" or some such, because of the compound growth of those first years.  Which, of course, just adds pressure, especially if you make choices as an 18 and 21 year old that mean you don't enter the labour market until you are nearly 25.</p>
<p class='text'>Comparing products is both easy and impossible.  You can look at what they have done historically; you can look at their investment strategies; you can look at the options they provide.  I eventually used this to decide that the product I wanted should have a flexible strategy which enabled me to keep my money in the tax-free shelter while allowing me to change the funds it was invested in.  It turned out that ruled out about half of them, but nothing like enough to make a decision.  On the other hand, if you look to the future, all pensions are required by law to give predictions based on the same projected growth rates (which have been nowhere close to the mark either in the '90s (much higher in reality) or since (much lower in reality)).  So, there's not a lot of help there, but it is at least possible.</p>
<p class='text'>Deciding how much to invest requires you to have a good idea of what your needs are going to be in 40 years' time.  Looking back (over the 33 that have passed, at least), it's fair to say that inflation has been a lot less than I anticipated, so the nominal sum I would have needed was very much less than I had anticipated.  But much more important than that, because of the limits of what you can pay into any pension scheme, the other investments I have made have really made the pension an irrelevance.</p>
<p class='text'>Oh, plus two years later, I left the UK and have not since had a job in the UK which has qualified me to make further contributions.  It is pretty much a white elephant.  And, having decided that flexibility was key, it turns out I have never moved my money between the various holding options in the pension.  In other words, I could have done nothing and I would have turned out just as well.</p>
<p class='text'>The most interesting thing about getting married - to me, at least - is that you know, going in, that "it is not going to be like this forever", but you don't know <i>what it is going to be like</i>.</p>
<p class='text'>And it seemed to me the most important quality that anyone should be looking for in a spouse is the ability to talk, plan, strategize and correct course as you sailed on the stormy seas of life.  I think any two people who meet by chance on a Saturday evening in Liverpool are just "ships passing in the night".  I don't know if anyone has ever done a study on how many "ships" we pass in the course of our lives, but I know there were forty other people there that night of whom I have seen about three this millenium.  By and large, people drift in and out of our lives, and it takes an act of considerable will to sail your ship alongside somebody else's - <i>and for them to be motivated to do the same</i>.</p>
<p class='text'>Sailing does feel like an excellent analogy for this.  You do have some level of control over how you steer your ship, and can choose what you would <i>like</i> to do in life.  Your spouse has her own ship and has the same choices: she can make the same choices - or different ones.  You can sail different routes for a while, and then come back together; or you can wake up one morning realizing you are on different oceans and there really isn't any point carrying on.  Sudden storms can whip up, and you can try and stay together; or you can choose the easy option of running before the storm and seeing where you end up.  I think it's fair to say that we've done some of all of these at various points in our marriage.</p>
<p class='text'>It seems tempting to say, starting out, "we want the same things: here's what we're going to do", but I don't think that even begins to take into account the "storms" that come your way.  Within a year of getting married, we had moved to California, I had a new job, my wife was unable to work (because of visa restrictions) and we had a child.  We spent our first wedding anniversary comparing cots in Target and K-Mart while our daughter howled.  I think it's fair to say that when we got married, I did not see any of that coming.  And that is just one year.</p>
<p class='text'>The key thing is continually adapting to the situations that arise, talking about them and correcting course: you can either see "storms" as something on the outside, that you (collectively) have to deal with, or as something that is a "problem" caused by your partner.  How you react tells you a lot about you, and about how you view your marriage.</p>
<p class='text'>I am not going to say that "walking away" is "wrong".  Like everything else, deciding whether to continue or quit is a balancing act between competing forces.  But, especially once you have children, ripping a marriage apart is a painful process for everyone involved.  I don't know how close we've come, but I do know that is has always been worth sticking out the storms, mending our boats together, and sailing on.</p>
<p class='text'>I still don't know where the journey will take us, but for now think of us on an ocean somewhere, chasing the sunset ...</p>
<p class='text'>For full disclosure, I did end up marrying the most beautiful woman who ever talked to me, and bought a random pension.  It's hard to kick against the pricks.</p>
<p class='text'>Oh, and I don't think I'm as smart as I used to think I was.  Even if my son is correct that I still think I'm "the smartest person in the room".</p>
